xes a bug regarding the hashing of source claims for input wires with multiple outs (that would be subject to a zero-check, not just verifier-side evaluation.)\nL174: \nL175: * * *\nL176: \nL177: Note\nL178: High Risk\nL179: Changes GKR proving-schedule shape and Fiat–Shamir transcript inputs, which can break proof compatibility and affect soundness if binding rules are wrong.\nL180: \nL181: Overview\nL182: GKR default scheduler now batches contiguous same-degree zero-check wires into one `GkrSumcheckLevel` with multiple claim groups (e.g. MiMC multi-source inputs at depth 3), via `batchForWire` and an extended loop in `DefaultProvingSchedule`.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nmod.rs - source (https://docs.rs/p3-sumcheck/latest/src/p3_sumcheck/zk/mod.rs.html)\nciteturn1view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://docs.rs/p3-sumcheck/latest/src/p3_sumcheck/zk/mod.rs.html\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 165\nL0: 【0†iframe】【1†Skip to main content】\nL1: \nL2: ## Files\nL3: #\nL4: \nL5: p3_sumcheck/zk/\nL6: mod.rs\nL7: \nL8:     `【2†1】//! Honest-verifier zero-knowledge sumcheck for WHIR.\nL9:     【3†2】//!\nL10:     【4†3】//! Implements Construction 6.3 of eprint 2026/391 on top of both\nL11:     【5†4】//! stacked-binding modes — prefix and suffix.\nL12:     【6†5】//!\nL13:     【7†6】//! # Overview\nL14:     【8†7】//!\nL15:     【9†8】//! The plain WHIR sumcheck leaks the witness:\nL16:     【10†9】//! Each round polynomial coefficient is an affine function of the secret message.\nL17:     【11†10】//!\nL18:     【12†11】//! The HVZK variant blinds those coefficients with `k` small mask polynomials, one per sumcheck round.\nL19:     【13†12】//! Overhead: only `O(k * ell_zk)` extra field elements.\nL20:     【14†13】//!\nL21:     【15†14】//! # Protocol shape\nL22:     【16†15】//!\nL23:     【17†16】//! Let `k` be the folding factor and `ell_zk` the mask code message length.\nL24:     【18†17】//!\nL25:     【19†18】//! 1. Prover samples `k` univariate masks of degree `ell_zk - 1` over the extension field.\nL26:     【20†19】//! 2. The masks are batch encoded under a zero-knowledge code, MMCS-committed\nL27:     【21†20】//!    as one matrix, and absorbed into the Fiat-Shamir transcript.\nL28:     【22†21】//! 3. Prover sends `mu_tilde`, the sum of all mask evaluations over `{0,1}^k`.\nL29:     【23†22】//! 4. Verifier samples a combining challenge `eps` in the extension field.\nL30:     【24†23】//! 5. Per-round wire polynomial mixes:\nL31:     【25†24】//!    - the live mask,\nL32:     【26†25】//!    - past mask evaluations at sampled challenges,\nL33:     【27†26】//!    - future-mask endpoints,\nL34:     【28†27】//!    - the plain sumcheck contribution scaled by `eps`.\nL35:     【29†28】//!\nL36:     【30†29】//! # Round polynomial at a glance\nL37:     【31†30】//!\nL38:     【32†31】//! For round `j` with past challenges `gamma_1, ..., gamma_{j - 1}`:\nL39:     【33†32】//!\nL40:     【34†33】//! ```text\nL41:     【35†34】//!     h_j(X) = 2^{k - j}     * s_j(X)\nL42:     【36†35】//!            + 2^{k - j}     * sum_{l <  j} s_l(gamma_l)\nL43:     【37†36】//!            + 2^{k - j - 1} * sum_{l >  j} ( s_l(0) + s_l(1) )\nL44:     【38†37】//!            + eps           * plain_piece_j(X)\nL45:     【39†38】//! ```\nL46:     【40†39】//!\nL47:     【41†40】//! Verifier checks:\nL48:     【42†41】//!\nL49:     【43†42】//! ```text\nL50:     【44†43】//!     round 1  : h_1(0) + h_1(1) = eps * mu + mu_tilde\nL51:     【45†44】//!     round j>1: h_j(0) + h_j(1) = h_{j - 1}(gamma_{j - 1})\nL52:     【46†45】//! ```\nL53:     【47†46】//!\nL54:     【48†47】//! # Module layout\nL55:     【49†48】//!\nL56:     【50†49】//! - Proof record and mask oracle handle.\nL57:     【51†50】//! - Fiat-Shamir description of one masked batch, driven by every party.\nL58:     【52†51】//! - Prover-side sumcheck with mask sampling and round-polynomial assembly.\nL59:     【53†52】//! - Verifier-side replay with the dropped-coefficient reconstruction.\nL60:     【54†53】//! - Witness-free simulators, one per prelude, used to prove honest-verifier zero-knowledge.\nL61:     【55†54】//!\nL62:     【56†55】//! # Two preludes, and the scope of each\nL63:     【57†56】//!\nL64:     【58†57】//! A masked batch reaches its target one of two ways, and the description names which:\nL65:     【59†58】//!\nL66:     【60†59】//! ```text\nL67:     【61†60】//!     recorded claims  ->  the target is derived from the claims a verifier recorded\nL68:     【62†61】//!     inherited claim  ->  the target arrives as public input and is bound first\nL69:     【63†62】//! ```\nL70:     【64†63】//!\nL71:     【65†64】//! The composed WHIR pipeline plays the inherited one, at every batch, on both sides.\nL72:     【66†65】//!\nL73:     【67†66】//! The recorded-claims prelude is this crate's own standalone surface instead.\nL74:     【68†67】//! No in-tree pipeline plays it: its entry points serve callers who drive a masked batch directly, and it stays supported on that basis.\nL75:     【69†68】//!\nL76:     【70†69】//! Both preludes carry a witness-free simulator, so neither ships without its zero-knowledge argument.\nL77:     【71†70】//!\nL78:     【72†71】//! Scope, so a future reader can act on it: a third prelude owes a third simulator.\nL79:     【73†72】//! Retiring the recorded-claims prelude means retiring the public entry points that expose it, which is an API decision rather than a change inside this module.\nL80:     【74†73】//!\nL81:     【75†74】//! # Layout coverage\nL82:     【76†75】//!\nL83:     【77†76】//! Both stacked-binding modes are supported.\nL84:     【78†77】//! The masking layer is binding-agnostic; only the residual handoff differs between modes.\nL85:     【79†78】//! Resolves [Plonky3#1649](https://github.com/Plonky3/Plonky3/issues/1649).\nL86:     【80†79】//!\nL87:     【81†80】//! # Field constraints (Lemma 6.4)\nL88:     【82†81】//!\nL89:     【83†82】//! - Base field characteristic must not be `2`.\nL90:     【84†83】//! - Mask message length `ell_zk` must be at least `3`, so the mask (degree `ell_zk - 1`) covers the degree-2 plain round polynomial.\nL91:     【85†84】//!\nL92:     【86†85】//! Both are checked where the transcript description is built.\nL93:     【87†86】//!\nL94:     【88†87】//! A prover treats a violation as its own configuration bug.\nL95:     【89†88】//!\nL96:     【90†89】//! A verifier reports it.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSumcheck API Documentation | ICICLE Docs (https://dev.ingonyama.com/api/cpp/sumcheck)\nciteturn1view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://dev.ingonyama.com/api/cpp/sumcheck\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 246\nL0: 【0†Skip to main content】\nL1: \nL2: 🚀 We just released ICICLE v4 — featuring a more intuitive API with lattices. 【1†Check out the migration guide】.\nL3: \nL4:   * 【2†4.0.0】\nL5:   * 【3†3.9.0】\nL6:   * 【4†3.8.0】\nL7:   * 【5†3.7.0】\nL8:   * 【6†3.6.0】\nL9:   * 【7†3.5.0】\nL10:   * 【8†3.4.0】\nL11:   * 【9†3.3.0】\nL12:   * 【10†3.2.0】\nL13:   * 【11†3.1.0】\nL14:   * 【12†3.0.0】\nL15:   * 【13†2.8.0】\nL16:   * 【14†1.10.1】\nL17: \nL18: Search\nL19: \nL20:   * 【15†API Overview】\nL21:   * 【16†C++】\nL22:     * 【16†C++ Overview】\nL23:     * 【17†MSM】\nL24:     * 【18†NTT / ECNTT】\nL25:     * 【19†Vector operations】\nL26:     * 【20†Program】\nL27:     * 【21†Polynomials】\nL28:     * 【22†Hash】\nL29:     * 【23†Merkle-Tree】\nL30:     * 【2†Sumcheck】\nL31:     * 【24†FRI】\nL32:     * 【25†Pairings】\nL33:     * 【26†PQC ML-KEM】\nL34:     * 【27†Serialization】\nL35:     * 【28†Matrix Operations】\nL36:   * 【29†Golang Bindings】\nL37:     * 【29†Golang Overview】\nL38:     * 【30†MSM】\nL39:     * 【31†MSM pre-computation】\nL40:     * 【32†NTT】\nL41:     * 【33†EC-NTT】\nL42:     * 【34†Vector operations】\nL43:     * 【35†Multi GPU Support】\nL44:     * 【36†Hash】\nL45:     * 【37†Merkle-Tree】\nL46:     * 【38†PQC ML-KEM】\nL47:   * 【39†Rust Bindings】\nL48:     * 【39†Rust Overview】\nL49:     * 【40†MSM】\nL50:     * 【41†NTT】\nL51:     * 【42†ECNTT】\nL52:     * 【43†Vector operations】\nL53:     * 【44†Program】\nL54:     * 【45†Polynomials】\nL55:     * 【46†Hash】\nL56:     * 【47†Merkle-Tree】\nL57:     * 【48†Sumcheck】\nL58:     * 【49†FRI】\nL59:     * 【50†Lattice SNARKs】\nL60:     * 【51†PQC ML-KEM】\nL61:     * 【52†Serialization】\nL62:     * 【53†Pairings】\nL63:     * 【54†Matrix Operations】\nL64: \nL65: Version: 4.0.0\nL66: \nL67: [Button: On this page]\nL68: # Sumcheck API Documentation\nL69: ## Overview【55†​】\nL70: \nL71: The Sumcheck protocol allows a Prover to prove to a Verifier that the sum of a multilinear polynomial, over the Boolean hypercube, equals a specific scalar value.\nL72: \nL73: For a polynomial $P$P with $n$n variables, the Prover aims to prove that:\nL74: \nL75: $$\\sum_{X_1 \\in \\{0, 1\\}}\\sum_{X_2 \\in \\{0, 1\\}}\\cdot\\cdot\\cdot\\sum_{X_n \\in \\{0, 1\\}} P(X_1, X_2,..., X_n) = C,$$X 1​∈{0,1}∑​X 2​∈{0,1}∑​⋅⋅⋅X n​∈{0,1}∑​P(X 1​,X 2​,...,X n​)=C,\nL76: \nL77: where $C$C is some scalar.\nL78: The proof is constructed interactively, involving a series of $n$n rounds. Each round consists of a challenge (from the Verifier), a computation (from the Prover), and a verification step (by the Verifier). Using a Fiat-Shamir (FS) scheme, the proof becomes non-interactive, enabling the Prover to generate the entire proof and send it to the Verifier for validation.\nL79: ### Sumcheck with Combine Function【56†​】\nL80: \nL81: The Sumcheck protocol can be generalized to handle multiple multilinear polynomials. In this case, assuming there are $m$m polynomials of $n$n variables, and $f$f is an arbitrary function that takes these $m$m polynomials and returns a polynomial of equal or higher degree, the Prover tries to prove that:\nL82: $$\\sum_{X_1 \\in \\{0, 1\\}}\\sum_{X_2 \\in \\{0, 1\\}}\\cdot\\cdot\\cdot\\sum_{X_n \\in \\{0, 1\\}} f\\left(P_1(X_1, ..., X_n), P_2(X_1, ..., X_n), ..., P_m(X_1, ..., X_n)\\right) = C,$$X 1​∈{0,1}∑​X 2​∈{0,1}∑​⋅⋅⋅X n​∈{0,1}∑​f(P 1​(X 1​,...,X n​),P 2​(X 1​,...,X n​),...,P m​(X 1​,...,X n​))=C,\nL83: \nL84: where $C$C is some scalar.\nL85: ## ICICLE's Sumecheck Implementation【57†​】\nL86: \nL87: ICICLE implements a non-interactive Sumcheck protocol that supports a combine function. It is available on both the CPU and CUDA backends of ICICLE. The polynomials are passed to the protocol in MLE (evaluation representation) form.\nL88: ### Implementation Limitations【58†​】\nL89: \nL90: There are some limitations and assumptions in the Sumcheck implementation:\nL91:   * The maximum size of the polynomials (i.e., the number of evaluations $2^n$2 n) depends on the number of polynomials and the memory size of the device (e.g., CPU/GPU) being used. For example, with 4 polynomials, a GPU with 24GB of memory should be able to handle Sumcheck for polynomials of size up to $2^29$2 2 9.\nL92:   * The polynomial size must a power of 2.\nL93:   * The current implementation does not support generating the challenge ($\\alpha$α) in an extension field. This functionality is necessary for ensuring security when working with small fields.\nL94: ## C++ API【59†​】\nL95: \nL96: A Sumcheck object can be created using the following function:\nL97: \nL98:     `Sumcheck<scalar_t> create_sumcheck()\nL99:     `\nL100: \nL101: There are two key configuration structs related to the Sumcheck protocol.\nL102: ### SumcheckConfig【60†​】\nL103: \nL104: The `SumcheckConfig` struct is used to specify parameters for the Sumcheck protocol. It contains the following fields:\nL105:   * `stream: icicleStreamHandle`: The CUDA stream for asynchronous execution. If `nullptr`, the default stream is used.\nL106:   * `use_extension_field: bool`: If true, an extension field is used for Fiat-Shamir results. Currently unsupported (should always be false).\nL107:   * `are_inputs_on_device: bool`: If true, the input polynomials are expected to reside on the device (e.g., GPU); otherwise, they are expected to reside on the host (e.g., CPU).\nL108:   * `is_async: bool`: If true runs the hash asynchronously.\nL109:   * `ext: ConfigExtension*`: Backend-specific extensions.\nL110: The default values are:\nL111: \nL112:     `  struct SumcheckConfig {\nL113:         icicleStreamHandle stream = nullptr;\nL114:         bool use_extension_field = false;\nL115:         uint64_t batch = 1;\nL116:         bool are_inputs_on_device =false;\nL117:         bool is_async = false;\nL118:         ConfigExtension* ext = nullptr;\nL119:       };\nL120:     `\n"
